1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,841
There are only four ways to lose
points on SAT systems questions.

2
00:00:03,861 --> 00:00:05,591
You solve for the wrong variable.

3
00:00:05,611 --> 00:00:07,397
You flip a sign when you substitute.

4
00:00:07,432 --> 00:00:08,948
You stop one step early.

5
00:00:08,968 --> 00:00:13,386
And the fourth one looks completely
right until you check one last number.

6
00:00:13,406 --> 00:00:16,727
The first three are quick to fix, and
you'll have them down in a few minutes.

7
00:00:17,191 --> 00:00:21,120
The fourth is calling a system
infinitely many when it has no solution.

8
00:00:21,227 --> 00:00:22,779
That's question five.

9
00:00:22,799 --> 00:00:26,655
Stay for it, because the equations
really do look like multiples.

10
00:00:26,747 --> 00:00:29,835
The method is the same every
time: circle what they ask for,

11
00:00:29,855 --> 00:00:32,033
then read the coefficients
before you solve.

12
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:35,513
Six practice questions, each
one harder than the last.

13
00:00:35,533 --> 00:00:38,390
Keep score as you go, and
see if you can get all six.

14
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:41,531
First, what a solution actually is.

15
00:00:42,495 --> 00:00:46,440
A solution to a system
is one pair of values,

16
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:50,960
an x and a y, that makes both
equations true at the same time.

17
00:00:51,009 --> 00:00:53,916
On a graph, each linear
equation is a line.

18
00:00:53,936 --> 00:00:56,009
The solution is where the two lines cross.

19
00:00:56,187 --> 00:00:58,524
That's why Desmos is so useful here.

20
00:00:58,544 --> 00:01:01,060
Type both equations,
click the intersection,

21
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,165
and read the point.

22
00:01:02,185 --> 00:01:04,220
By hand, you have two tools.

23
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:09,435
Substitution: solve one equation for
a variable and plug it into the other.

24
00:01:10,149 --> 00:01:14,720
Elimination: add or subtract the
equations so one variable cancels.

25
00:01:14,817 --> 00:01:16,154
Here's how to choose.

26
00:01:16,174 --> 00:01:19,011
If one equation already
says y equals something,

27
00:01:19,031 --> 00:01:21,389
or x equals something, substitute.

28
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:24,940
If a pair of coefficients
already match or are opposites,

29
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:25,995
eliminate.

30
00:01:26,174 --> 00:01:29,047
If neither is true, elimination
is usually faster anyway.

31
00:01:29,067 --> 00:01:30,853
You just multiply first.

32
00:01:30,897 --> 00:01:34,468
On the SAT, systems questions
come in four main types.

33
00:01:34,862 --> 00:01:37,484
Type one asks you to solve for x or y.

34
00:01:37,504 --> 00:01:40,683
Type two asks for a
combination, like x plus y.

35
00:01:40,703 --> 00:01:43,611
Type three asks how many
solutions there are,

36
00:01:43,631 --> 00:01:47,789
or for the value of a constant that
makes a system have no solution or

37
00:01:47,809 --> 00:01:49,024
infinitely many.

38
00:01:49,953 --> 00:01:54,167
Type four is a word problem, where
you build the system yourself.

39
00:01:54,632 --> 00:01:55,882
We'll do every type.

40
00:01:55,958 --> 00:01:57,030
Question one.

41
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:02,173
In the system, y equals three x minus two,

42
00:02:02,458 --> 00:02:06,423
and two x plus y equals thirteen.

43
00:02:06,743 --> 00:02:09,759
If x, y is the solution to the system,

44
00:02:09,779 --> 00:02:11,458
what is the value of x?

45
00:02:11,524 --> 00:02:12,631
Pause and try it.

46
00:02:15,846 --> 00:02:18,361
The first equation already
says y equals something.

47
00:02:18,381 --> 00:02:19,989
That's the signal to substitute.

48
00:02:21,203 --> 00:02:24,197
Replace y in the second
equation with three x minus two.

49
00:02:24,217 --> 00:02:26,911
Now it's one equation with one variable.

50
00:02:26,931 --> 00:02:32,395
Two x plus three x is five x, so
five x minus two equals thirteen.

51
00:02:32,466 --> 00:02:36,287
Add two, and five x equals fifteen.

52
00:02:36,467 --> 00:02:39,018
Divide by five, and x equals three.

53
00:02:39,038 --> 00:02:40,288
The answer is B.

54
00:02:40,337 --> 00:02:41,730
Look at the wrong choices.

55
00:02:42,015 --> 00:02:44,138
Fifteen is five x, one step too early.

56
00:02:44,158 --> 00:02:46,138
Seven is y, because three times three,

57
00:02:46,158 --> 00:02:47,210
minus two, is seven.

58
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:49,673
And eleven fifths comes from
flipping the sign on the two.

59
00:02:49,693 --> 00:02:53,300
Each one is a real number
from a slightly wrong process.

60
00:02:53,334 --> 00:02:54,334
Question two.

61
00:02:55,262 --> 00:03:01,226
Three x plus two y equals seventeen,
and five x minus two y equals seven.

62
00:03:02,048 --> 00:03:03,798
The solution is x, y.

63
00:03:03,941 --> 00:03:05,834
What is the value of y?

64
00:03:05,885 --> 00:03:06,992
Pause and try it.

65
00:03:10,206 --> 00:03:12,527
Neither equation is solved for a variable.

66
00:03:12,885 --> 00:03:16,722
But look at the y terms:
plus two y and minus two y.

67
00:03:16,742 --> 00:03:17,778
They're opposites.

68
00:03:18,312 --> 00:03:21,206
Add the equations, and y disappears.

69
00:03:21,253 --> 00:03:23,502
Three x plus five x is eight x.

70
00:03:23,646 --> 00:03:25,860
Seventeen plus seven is twenty-four.

71
00:03:25,968 --> 00:03:29,090
So eight x equals twenty-four,
and x equals three.

72
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:31,574
But the question asks for y.

73
00:03:31,681 --> 00:03:33,824
Plug three back into the first equation.

74
00:03:33,932 --> 00:03:37,447
Nine plus two y equals
seventeen, so two y equals eight,

75
00:03:37,467 --> 00:03:38,769
and y equals four.

76
00:03:38,789 --> 00:03:42,182
If you typed three, you answered
a question they didn't ask.

77
00:03:42,271 --> 00:03:43,378
Question three.

78
00:03:43,807 --> 00:03:49,307
Five x plus two y equals thirty-one, and
two x plus five y equals twenty-five.

79
00:03:49,736 --> 00:03:51,914
What is the value of x plus y?

80
00:03:51,970 --> 00:03:53,077
Pause and try it.

81
00:03:56,291 --> 00:03:58,270
When a question asks for a combination,

82
00:03:58,290 --> 00:04:00,970
don't rush to find x and y separately.

83
00:04:01,077 --> 00:04:02,664
Look at the coefficients first.

84
00:04:02,684 --> 00:04:05,878
They're flipped: five and
two, then two and five.

85
00:04:05,898 --> 00:04:08,362
When that happens, add the equations.

86
00:04:08,393 --> 00:04:10,678
Five x plus two x is seven x.

87
00:04:11,179 --> 00:04:13,587
Two y plus five y is seven y.

88
00:04:13,607 --> 00:04:15,766
And thirty-one plus
twenty-five is fifty-six.

89
00:04:15,786 --> 00:04:19,750
So seven x plus seven y equals fifty-six.

90
00:04:20,213 --> 00:04:23,607
Divide everything by seven,
and x plus y equals eight.

91
00:04:24,357 --> 00:04:26,286
The answer is C.

92
00:04:26,361 --> 00:04:28,861
Fifty-six is the trap
if you forget to divide.

93
00:04:29,111 --> 00:04:31,162
Two is what you get if you
subtract the equations.

94
00:04:31,182 --> 00:04:34,324
That gives x minus y.

95
00:04:34,432 --> 00:04:36,182
Useful, but not what they asked.

96
00:04:36,361 --> 00:04:38,646
And five is x by itself.

97
00:04:38,722 --> 00:04:39,901
Question four.

98
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:42,988
A school club sold one hundred
twenty tickets to a play.

99
00:04:43,008 --> 00:04:45,202
Student tickets cost six dollars each,

100
00:04:45,222 --> 00:04:47,401
and adult tickets cost ten dollars each.

101
00:04:47,544 --> 00:04:50,437
The club collected a total of
nine hundred forty dollars.

102
00:04:50,686 --> 00:04:53,187
How many adult tickets did the club sell?

103
00:04:53,267 --> 00:04:54,374
Pause and try it.

104
00:04:58,589 --> 00:05:00,303
Name the unknowns first.

105
00:05:00,553 --> 00:05:02,589
Let s be the number of student tickets,

106
00:05:02,839 --> 00:05:05,196
and a be the number of adult tickets.

107
00:05:06,124 --> 00:05:09,016
Word problems like this
usually give you two totals:

108
00:05:09,124 --> 00:05:11,909
a total count and a total amount of money.

109
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:14,589
Each total becomes one equation.

110
00:05:14,627 --> 00:05:18,520
The count equation is s plus
a equals one hundred twenty.

111
00:05:18,555 --> 00:05:22,698
The money equation is six s plus
ten a equals nine hundred forty.

112
00:05:22,841 --> 00:05:25,555
Each kind of ticket gets
multiplied by its price.

113
00:05:25,596 --> 00:05:27,167
Solve the first one for s.

114
00:05:27,595 --> 00:05:30,096
s equals one hundred twenty minus a.

115
00:05:30,381 --> 00:05:34,147
Substitute: six times the quantity
one hundred twenty minus a,

116
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:37,274
plus ten a, equals nine hundred forty.

117
00:05:37,416 --> 00:05:41,524
That's seven hundred twenty plus
four a equals nine hundred forty.

118
00:05:41,569 --> 00:05:45,442
So four a equals two hundred
twenty, and a equals fifty-five.

119
00:05:45,462 --> 00:05:48,633
The answer is A.

120
00:05:48,653 --> 00:05:50,703
Sixty-five is the number
of student tickets,

121
00:05:50,723 --> 00:05:53,689
the trap for solving
for the wrong variable.

122
00:05:54,153 --> 00:05:58,116
In Desmos, let x be student
tickets and y be adult tickets.

123
00:05:58,759 --> 00:06:02,153
Type both equations, zoom out,
and click the intersection:

124
00:06:02,473 --> 00:06:03,474
sixty-five, fifty-five.

125
00:06:04,112 --> 00:06:05,720
Now type three.

126
00:06:05,755 --> 00:06:08,220
How many solutions does a system have?

127
00:06:08,398 --> 00:06:10,470
Two lines can only do three things.

128
00:06:10,541 --> 00:06:12,914
They cross once: one solution.

129
00:06:12,934 --> 00:06:14,970
They're parallel: no solution.

130
00:06:15,005 --> 00:06:17,720
Or they're the same line: infinitely many.

131
00:06:17,807 --> 00:06:20,215
You can tell which one without graphing.

132
00:06:20,235 --> 00:06:22,485
Write both equations in the same form,

133
00:06:22,771 --> 00:06:24,858
then compare the coefficients as ratios:

134
00:06:24,878 --> 00:06:27,985
x to x, y to y, and constant to constant.

135
00:06:28,027 --> 00:06:32,170
If the x and y ratios are
different, there's one solution.

136
00:06:32,527 --> 00:06:36,170
If the x and y ratios match
but the constants don't,

137
00:06:36,384 --> 00:06:39,276
the lines are parallel,
so there's no solution.

138
00:06:39,670 --> 00:06:43,043
If all three ratios
match, it's the same line,

139
00:06:43,063 --> 00:06:44,670
so there are infinitely many.

140
00:06:44,698 --> 00:06:45,984
Question five.

141
00:06:46,234 --> 00:06:48,591
How many solutions does this system have?

142
00:06:49,127 --> 00:06:54,304
Six x minus four y equals ten, and
nine x minus six y equals twelve.

143
00:06:54,357 --> 00:06:55,464
Pause and try it.

144
00:06:58,679 --> 00:06:59,929
Compare the ratios.

145
00:07:00,393 --> 00:07:02,215
Six over nine is two thirds.

146
00:07:02,322 --> 00:07:04,786
Negative four over negative
six is also two thirds.

147
00:07:04,857 --> 00:07:06,750
So the lines have the same slope.

148
00:07:07,071 --> 00:07:08,357
Now the constants.

149
00:07:09,036 --> 00:07:11,837
Ten over twelve is five
sixths, and that doesn't match.

150
00:07:11,857 --> 00:07:13,766
Same slope, different lines.

151
00:07:13,786 --> 00:07:16,143
They're parallel, so there's no solution.

152
00:07:16,215 --> 00:07:17,607
The answer is A.

153
00:07:17,646 --> 00:07:19,967
The trap is infinitely many.

154
00:07:20,718 --> 00:07:24,305
If you only check the x and y terms,
the equations look like multiples.

155
00:07:24,325 --> 00:07:26,126
Always check the constants too.

156
00:07:26,146 --> 00:07:28,269
And choice C is out from the start.

157
00:07:28,289 --> 00:07:30,932
Two different lines can
never cross exactly twice.

158
00:07:30,991 --> 00:07:33,740
Question six, the hardest one.

159
00:07:33,812 --> 00:07:38,634
In the system, one half x
plus two thirds y equals four,

160
00:07:38,704 --> 00:07:42,026
and k x plus eight y equals eleven.

161
00:07:42,491 --> 00:07:43,669
k is a constant.

162
00:07:44,919 --> 00:07:48,134
For what value of k does
the system have no solution?

163
00:07:48,198 --> 00:07:49,036
Pause and try it.

164
00:07:49,056 --> 00:07:51,627
Take a little longer on this one.

165
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:58,129
No solution means parallel lines.

166
00:07:58,558 --> 00:08:01,181
So the x and y coefficients
have to be in the same ratio,

167
00:08:01,201 --> 00:08:03,681
but the constants can't be.

168
00:08:03,701 --> 00:08:06,896
The fractions make that
hard to see, so clear them.

169
00:08:06,916 --> 00:08:09,380
Multiply the whole first
equation by twelve.

170
00:08:09,442 --> 00:08:11,370
Twelve times one half is six.

171
00:08:11,977 --> 00:08:14,263
Twelve times two thirds is eight.

172
00:08:14,799 --> 00:08:16,870
Twelve times four is forty-eight.

173
00:08:17,406 --> 00:08:21,584
So the first equation becomes six
x plus eight y equals forty-eight.

174
00:08:21,733 --> 00:08:23,876
Now both equations have eight y.

175
00:08:24,091 --> 00:08:27,463
For the lines to be parallel, the
x coefficients have to match too,

176
00:08:27,483 --> 00:08:28,948
so k equals six.

177
00:08:29,555 --> 00:08:30,841
Check the constants.

178
00:08:31,090 --> 00:08:32,963
Forty-eight and eleven are different,

179
00:08:32,983 --> 00:08:34,678
so the lines never touch.

180
00:08:34,698 --> 00:08:36,055
The answer is six.

181
00:08:36,104 --> 00:08:37,283
Desmos shortcut.

182
00:08:37,853 --> 00:08:39,353
Type both equations.

183
00:08:40,033 --> 00:08:43,440
For the fractions, press the right
arrow after each denominator,

184
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:45,997
so x and y don't end up on the bottom.

185
00:08:46,175 --> 00:08:48,460
Desmos offers a slider for k.

186
00:08:48,711 --> 00:08:50,620
Drag it until the lines are parallel.

187
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:52,390
That happens at k equals six.

188
00:08:52,452 --> 00:08:53,452
One more note.

189
00:08:53,524 --> 00:08:56,182
If the question had asked for
infinitely many solutions,

190
00:08:56,202 --> 00:08:58,754
the constants would have to match too.

191
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:03,131
The second equation would need to be
six x plus eight y equals forty-eight.

192
00:09:03,702 --> 00:09:05,666
Same idea, one extra check.

193
00:09:05,702 --> 00:09:07,202
Here's the test-day method.

194
00:09:07,345 --> 00:09:10,737
Step one: circle what
the question asks for.

195
00:09:11,345 --> 00:09:13,559
x, y, x plus y, or a constant.

196
00:09:13,630 --> 00:09:15,880
That sets your goal
before you write anything.

197
00:09:15,952 --> 00:09:18,417
Step two: scan the coefficients.

198
00:09:18,809 --> 00:09:21,524
A variable already alone means substitute.

199
00:09:21,845 --> 00:09:24,523
Matching or opposite
coefficients mean eliminate.

200
00:09:24,809 --> 00:09:27,774
Flipped coefficients
mean add, then divide.

201
00:09:27,881 --> 00:09:31,631
And a question about the number
of solutions means compare ratios.

202
00:09:31,677 --> 00:09:34,263
Step three: if it's messy,
or you want a check,

203
00:09:34,283 --> 00:09:37,498
type both equations into Desmos
and click the intersection.

204
00:09:38,034 --> 00:09:43,140
For word problems, write down what x and
y stand for before you type anything.

205
00:09:43,214 --> 00:09:44,286
Quick recap.

206
00:09:44,857 --> 00:09:47,107
A solution is where the lines cross.

207
00:09:47,429 --> 00:09:50,000
Substitute when a
variable is already alone.

208
00:09:50,463 --> 00:09:52,749
Eliminate when the coefficients line up.

209
00:09:53,071 --> 00:09:56,551
For x plus y, look for a
combination before you solve.

210
00:09:56,571 --> 00:09:59,159
And to count solutions, compare ratios,

211
00:09:59,179 --> 00:10:00,607
including the constants.

212
00:10:00,658 --> 00:10:04,479
Every question here was written for this
video in the style of the real test.

213
00:10:04,622 --> 00:10:08,351
For official practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter by

214
00:10:08,371 --> 00:10:11,515
skill, including systems
of two linear equations.

215
00:10:11,544 --> 00:10:13,579
If this helped, subscribe.

216
00:10:15,043 --> 00:10:18,559
The next video is quadratics: the vertex,

217
00:10:18,579 --> 00:10:20,508
the roots, and the discriminant.
